WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:11.410
One day in physics class, you’re learning
about gravity, and your friend tells you that

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:17.800
they exert a stronger gravitational force
on you than the nearest star beyond our sun,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.320
proxima centauri.

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:26.250
You have a mass of sixty kilograms, as does
your friend, who is sitting two meters away.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:33.730
Proxima centauri has a mass of 2.45 times
ten to the twenty-nine kilograms, and it is

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:38.120
4.01 times ten to the sixteen meters away.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:40.020
So is your friend correct?

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.520
Which exerts more gravity on you, your friend,
or the star?

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:49.050
To answer this, we will need to use Newton’s
law of universal gravitation, so if you missed

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.720
that tutorial, check it out right now.

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.620
If you know what to do, give this calculation
a try.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.700
This is easy to solve if we know this equation.

00:01:20.719 --> 00:01:27.240
All we do is plug in the masses and distances
involved, as well as the gravitational constant.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.219
First, for your friend, we plug in sixty and
sixty as the masses, and two meters as the

00:01:33.219 --> 00:01:34.979
distance between you.

00:01:34.979 --> 00:01:41.649
We square that and evaluate, and we should
get six times ten to the negative eight Newtons.

00:01:41.649 --> 00:01:47.289
This is an extremely tiny force, which explains
why people don’t slowly drift towards each

00:01:47.289 --> 00:01:51.319
other when sitting in a room.

00:01:51.319 --> 00:01:53.529
Now let’s do one for the star.

00:01:53.529 --> 00:01:58.990
We replace the mass of your friend with the
mass of the star, but we also change this

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:05.960
value to the distance to the star, so we have
a huge number on top, but we have a pretty

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:12.100
big number on the bottom, too, and we have
to square it, so when we evaluate this, we

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.640
get 6.1 times ten to the negative thirteen
Newtons.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:24.200
As small as the first number was, this one
is a hundred thousand times smaller, so it

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.150
looks like your friend was right, none of
the stars in the universe apart from the sun

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:34.500
can have any significant gravitational influence
on the earth.

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:36.160
Take that, astrology!

